<p>I&rsquo;m up again after snatching another hour of sleep or so. Before I go to the office, I thought I&rsquo;d ask the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blogosphere">blogosphere</a> (does anyone still use this term? Does it exist?) what makes a good blog good?</p>
<p>This is a question I kind of answered before, when I explained what blogs I usually add to <a href="https://taonaw.com/2024/04/30/the-return-of.html">my blogroll</a>. Two main things:</p>
<ol>
<li>A blog by one person, not a group (or a publication of sorts)</li>
<li>That person publishes posts regularly (once a week or so at least)</li>
</ol>
<p>These are good, but they are guidelines more than explanations. So what <em>is</em> &ldquo;good&rdquo; blog, anyway?</p>
<p>One of the first things that comes to mind is the quality of the writing. It&rsquo;s hard to pin down, because it can vary from those who write beautiful essays to those who prefer a quick list of links with explanations. Also, I tend to find that people who write about something they&rsquo;re passionate about are usually interesting and the writing quality takes a second or even a third place, if they include photos or something else that presents what they do.</p>
<p>Writing is still important. It&rsquo;s not about grammar and typos (though those have their respectful plce) as much as it is about style and confidence. I&rsquo;m not sure how to put it into the right words, but you can tell when you read a blog by someone who writes often. I think some popular bloggers &ldquo;wrote it until they made it,&rdquo; in a way. They&rsquo;ve been at it for long enough to know how to write well enough, and their style is prominent. You know it&rsquo;s <em>there</em> when you read it.</p>
<p>Most bloggers use a service for their blog, myself included, which is why it&rsquo;s even more important that a &ldquo;good&rdquo; blog has a certain personality that shows through the service&rsquo;s default template. Certain visual tweaks, perhaps, or a logo, or some plugin that does something different beyond the usual things, like emails and comments. For example, <a href="https://sachachua.com/blog/">Sacha Chua</a> writes almost exclusively about Emacs and Lisp, but she sometimes includes sketches and mindmaps as the visual person she is. <a href="https://anniemueller.com/">Annie</a> has a rather simple Micro.blog-themed blog, but she has a Guestbook with sketches. <a href="https://sals.place/blog/">Sal</a>, who has a &ldquo;plain&rdquo;, simple-looking blog, writes about other things besides tech (even though he&rsquo;s a tech guy), showing his readers who he is as a person.</p>
<p>Then there&rsquo;s the matter of website maintenance itself. Not everyone&rsquo;s a techie, and that&rsquo;s good (I keep saying we need more non-techies to blog), but if someone has a blog, they should care about it. These days, this usually means a theme that looks OK on both a computer screen and a smartphone, an <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RSS">RSS</a> feed, and an ad-free experience. I would also like to say no heavy scripts loading in the background, but some folks who use certain services don&rsquo;t really have a choice; they just use what&rsquo;s given to them. Still, keeping links working, a good archive, a working search&hellip; in my mind, these are the equivalent of keeping your home clean and tidy, at least to a degree.</p>
<p>What do you think?</p>
